.TH HYPERFLUXCTL 1 2026-10-23 "HyperFlux Next V2 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hyperfluxctl \- inspect and troubleshoot Razer devices on a HyperFlux V2 receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hyperfluxctl
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hyperfluxctl
talks to
.B hyperflux\-next\-bridge
and looks at how the receiver's hidraw nodes, /dev/uhid, the device keeper
and the OpenRazer drivers are set up.
It changes nothing unless
.B doctor \-\-fix
asks first and gets a yes.
.PP
It connects to the per-user socket
.I /run/user/UID/hyperflux\-next\-bridge.sock
first and falls back to the system socket, which works for members of the
.B hyperflux
group.
Each run uses its own client id, so several can run at once.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Driver, bridge, device keeper, number of receivers, one line per device,
and the next thing to do.
The driver line says how the bridge reaches the receiver:
.BR active ,
.BR "no receiver" ,
.BI "owned by " driver
(another driver holds an interface the bridge needs),
.BR "no hidraw node" ,
or
.B hidraw not open to the bridge and keeper
(the node's owner, group or mode is wrong).
The keeper line says whether
.B hyperflux\-next\-keeper.service
runs and the bridge has it, as systemd reports it.
When Doctor only has warnings, the next thing is
.BR "no action required" ,
with the number of warnings.
.TP
.B devices
One block per device.
It has the ids, the receiver and what named its devices (its paired slots
or its pairing inventory), lane, support, presence, last contact, battery
and its age, and which program controls the device.
It also says whether the device's slot reports it connected through the
receiver, and what became of its OpenRazer device
.RI ( live ,
.IR "on its cable" ,
or why not).
Then comes how long the bridge waits after sending to the device before
the first read, learned from the catalog's value, for example
.IR "wait 3.0 ms (learned from 3.0)" ,
with
.I resumed at 5.0
added when learning went on from a wait kept from the bridge's last run.
Last are the device's exchange counts since the bridge started: how many,
how many were answered at the first read or not at all, and how often the
bridge read again after another reply or a busy one.
A device the receiver hasn't named yet is listed as an
.I Unidentified pointer device
or
.IR "Unidentified keyboard device" ,
with its battery only, until moving or waking it makes the receiver name it.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
each device also lists, with counts, the commands the bridge refused
because they are missing from the device's list.
.TP
.BR battery " [" \-\-waybar ]
One battery line per device.
When the bridge has no way to learn whether a device is charging, its
line says
.IR "charging not reported" ,
and a device that answered that it can't report its level says
.IR "not reported" .
With
.BR \-\-waybar ,
one JSON object with
.BR text ,
.BR tooltip ,
.B class
and
.B percentage
for a bar's custom module.
It is printed even when the bridge can't be reached.
The class is never
.B low
or
.B critical
while the level is rising.
.TP
.B watch
Prints a device line whenever that device changes.
Runs until interrupted and reconnects when the bridge restarts.
.TP
.BR doctor " [" \-\-explain "] [" \-\-fix " [" \-\-yes ]]
Checks what is left of the 2.x driver, the receiver's hidraw nodes,
/dev/uhid, the bridge and its device keeper, and each device's battery
entry.
When the OpenRazer integration is installed, it checks that as well.
Then it explains the first problem it finds.
Warnings, such as a uaccess ACL on /dev/uhid, count as findings too.
When there is nothing but warnings, it says HyperFlux Next V2 is ready,
gives the number of warnings and explains the first one.
.B \-\-explain
explains every finding.
.B \-\-fix
shows the safe fixes as exact commands and asks before running each one.
Without a terminal it just lists them.
Fixes that need root run through a visible
.B sudo
and are always asked about.
.B \-\-yes
skips the question only for fixes that run as yourself.
.TP
\fBbench\fR [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIid\fR] [\fB\-\-delay\-us\fR \fIn\fR]... [\fB\-\-retry\-us\fR \fIn\fR]... [\fB\-\-iterations\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-query firmware\fR|\fBbattery\fR] [\fB\-\-reread\-busy\fR [\fB\-\-quick\fR]] [\fB\-\-defaults\fR]
Measures how long a read-only query takes with shorter waits before the
first read (default 8000 6000 5000 4000 3000 microseconds, 20 runs each).
It measures every online device, or just the one
.B \-\-device
names.
.B \-\-iterations
sets the number of runs, from 1 to 1000.
Each device's table is headed with the wait the bridge uses for it
outside bench.
Answers other than a success are counted by the receiver's status:
.BR busy ,
.BR failed ,
.B no response/asleep
and
.BR "not supported" .
.I without an answer
means nothing that matched came back.
Nothing on the devices or in the bridge's settings is changed.
.B \-\-retry\-us
(1000 to 10000, repeatable) also tries other waits between reads after
a read that found no answer, instead of the 1 ms the bridge uses outside
bench.
The table then has a row for each first wait and retry interval, with the
median, p90 and maximum time and the reads per exchange.
Whatever the interval, the reads end 40 ms after the first.
A shorter interval just fits more of them in.
.B \-\-reread\-busy
reads again after a busy answer, as the bridge does outside bench, at the
retry interval and within the same time. Without it bench takes busy as
the answer, which shows how often a device answers busy at each wait.
Its defaults are first waits of 1000 2000 3000 4000 6000 and retry
intervals of 1000 2000 5000 10000 microseconds, and the table gets a
.B busy
column for the exchanges still busy at the end.
.B \-\-quick
cuts that to first waits of 2000 3000 4000 and retry intervals of 1000
and 2000.
.B \-\-defaults
measures each device at the timings the bridge uses for it now: its
learned first wait and 1 ms between reads, reading on after busy answers.
It prints one row per device with the successful runs, the median, p90 and
maximum time and the reads per exchange.
It doesn't combine with
.BR \-\-delay\-us ,
.BR \-\-retry\-us ,
.B \-\-reread\-busy
or
.BR \-\-quick .
.TP
\fBcapture\fR [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-output\fR \fIpath\fR]
Records receiver reports for
.I n
seconds (default 60) into a JSON lines file readable only by you, to help
add support for a new device.
It starts with the receiver's pairing inventory.
Of that it keeps the first 64 bytes, the length, the offset where the
bridge found the marker and the product ids, and nothing that could tell
one receiver from another.
Vendor reports are recorded as they came, up to 16 bytes each.
Key presses and pointer movement are recorded as activity, without their
contents.
Receiver ids become
.BR receiver\-1 ,
.B receiver\-2
and so on, as in
.BR report .
An existing file is never overwritten.
.TP
\fBstall\-check\fR [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-threshold\-ms\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-ab\fR]
Listens to the receiver's pointer input for
.I n
seconds (default 60) while you move the mouse in circles.
It reports the report rate, the time between reports (p50, p99, maximum)
and every stall: a gap longer than the threshold (default 50 ms) in the
middle of movement.
It finds the input devices in sysfs (those of USB 1532:00cf with REL_X and
REL_Y), opens them read-only and never grabs them, so the desktop gets
every event.
Its own copy of the events is stamped with CLOCK_BOOTTIME, the bridge's
clock.
Reading them needs the
.B input
group or root.
Without either it says so and exits 3.
.IP
The rules: a gap is a stall when at least 5 motion reports come in the
100 ms before it and 5 in the 100 ms after it (the first report after the
gap left out), and one side's distance is at most 3 times the other's.
Gaps over 1 s are pauses.
Gaps where the kernel dropped events are left out.
.IP
Each stall is listed with what overlapped it.
Replies in the bridge's capture stream count if they ended between the
start of the gap and 60 ms after its end, since an exchange takes at most
that long.
Each one shows its lane and class/command, and whether the bridge, an app
or this check asked.
The kernel's USB, HID and suspend lines (from
.BR "journalctl \-k" )
within 250 ms of it are listed too.
The bridge records replies to queries only, so writes such as lighting
changes can't be matched.
It also says how much of the run had an exchange under way, which gives
the number of overlaps chance alone would produce.
The verdict says the queries can delay movement when at least two stalls
overlap traffic and at least twice as many as chance would give.
That is correlation, not proof.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-ab ,
the first half just listens.
In the second, this check sends a read-only battery query to the mouse
every 500 ms through bench (a device mode query, then 07/80).
Each query takes a lease of its own and uses the wait the bridge has
learned for the mouse.
The halves are compared by stalls per minute of movement.
They count as different when a one-sided binomial test of how the stalls
split between them, weighted by each half's movement, is below 5%.
Less than 5 s of movement, in the run or in either half, is too little to
tell.
.TP
.B probe receiver
For maintainers.
Has the bridge ask each receiver its fixed read-only questions about the
paired devices (00:bf on transaction ids 0x08, 0x88 and 0x3f, and 00:c5 on
0x1f, which other Razer receivers answer).
It prints each raw answer with its status, data size and first 16 data
bytes, or
.IR "no answer" ,
and the first 22 bytes of the pairing inventory.
The bridge asks 00:bf on 0x08 and 0x3f itself, to name the devices.
.TP
\fBreport\fR [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-output\fR \fIpath\fR]
Makes one complete device report in a single JSON file, to send with a
request to support an untested device.
The file is readable only by you and never overwrites an existing one.
It holds
.B devices
in JSON, a guided capture that prompts you through using the device for
.I n
seconds (default 180: idle, then use it, a mouse on and off its mat, the
cable in and out, and sleep and wake),
.BR "bench \-\-defaults \-\-iterations 200" ,
.BR doctor ,
.B "probe receiver"
and the bridge's and the keeper's journal lines for this boot about the
OpenRazer side, refused commands, serial reads, the cable twin and the
keeper's connection.
It masks receiver ids and input link names, and Doctor's suggested commands
in it say
.B $USER
and
.BR "$(id \-u)" ,
as in
.BR support\-bundle .
When the journal can't be read it says so rather than leaving it out.
.TP
\fBsupport\-bundle\fR [\fB\-\-preview\fR] [\fB\-\-output\fR \fIpath\fR]
Writes one JSON file for a bug report, readable only by you and never
overwriting an existing file.
It holds versions, driver and bridge state, Doctor's findings and device
models, but no serial numbers, receiver ids, input link names, reports or
logs.
Doctor's suggested commands in it say
.B $USER
and
.B $(id \-u)
where they need your login name or user id.
.B \-\-preview
shows what would be written and writes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON document instead of text.
.B watch
prints one object per line.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Talk to the bridge on
.I path
only.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Print the usage or the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Fine.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, or the command failed.
.TP
.B 2
Doctor found something: a problem, or only warnings.
.B status
exits 0 even when Doctor would find something.
.B stall\-check
exits 2 when it found stalls, and 1 when there was too little movement.
.TP
.B 3
.B stall\-check
can't read the receiver's input devices.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HYPERFLUX_NEXT_SOCKET
Use this socket instead of the per-user and system sockets.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/hyperflux\-next/bridge.sock
The bridge's system socket.
.TP
.I /run/hyperflux\-next/keeper.sock
Where the device keeper reaches the bridge.
Only the keeper's user may connect.
.SH EXAMPLES
A waybar module that shows battery levels:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
"custom/hyperflux": {
    "exec": "hyperfluxctl battery \-\-waybar",
    "return\-type": "json",
    "interval": 60,
    "format": "{}"
}
.EE
.in
.PP
The same for polybar, which takes plain text:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
[module/hyperflux]
type = custom/script
exec = hyperfluxctl battery \-\-waybar | jq \-r .text
interval = 60
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR upower (1)
